          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            = succeed "," do
              %strong topological vector space
            %strong TVS
            for short, is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+field"} topological field
            (usually a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field"} local field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            or the field of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            with the usual topology)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            (called the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topology
            for which the addition and scalar multiplication maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo :
                  %mi k
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +: X \times X \to X, \qquad \cdot: k \times X \to X
          %p
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TopVect"} TopVect
          %p
            Much as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            …
            %em provided
            that we use the
            %em two-sorted
            notion of vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k, X)
            so that the first sort is interpreted as the topological ground field. (More generally, there is a notion of topological module which is the internalization in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Top
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
            of the two-sorted notion of module involving a ring sort and a module over that ring; this notion is purely equational and thus interpretable in terms of finite products. Then a TVS is a topological module whose underlying ring is a field.) N.B.: if we internalize the one-sorted notion vector space, in which the ground field is encoded in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            as the set of unary operations, the continuity of the unary operations gives only a continuous map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|k|} \times X \to X
            where we use the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topology"} discrete topology
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            – which is not what we want.
          %p
            Like any topological
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            carries a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniform+space"} uniform space
            structure generated by a basis of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entourages"} entourages
            (aka vicinities) that correspond to neighborhoods
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mi u
                  %mo −
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{(u, v) \in X \times X: u - v \in U\}
          %p
            Thus many uniform notions (uniform continuity, completeness, etc.) carry over to the TVS context. Also from the uniformity (although it is also easy to prove directly), it follows that a TVS is completely regular, and also Hausdorff if and only if it is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axiom"} separation axiom
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_0
            condition to rule out degenerate cases, but that prevents the category of TVSes from being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+concrete+category"} topological
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is not Hausdorff, then the subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_0
            defined as the intersection of all neighborhoods of zero is a vector subspace of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+space"} quotient vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V/V_0
            is Hausdorff, hence Tihonov (= completely regular Hausdorff).
          %p
            The condition that scalar multiplication is continuous puts significant constraints on the topology of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            has a base of compact absorbing neighborhoods of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is Hausdorff, then for any non-zero
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in X
            the function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi v
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi k
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \- \cdot v
                  \\;\colon\;
                  k \longrightarrow V
          %p
            maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphically
            onto its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            cannot (for instance) be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            (unless it is the zero space and so has no non-zero
            = succeed ");" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            (over a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field"} local field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is finite-dimensional. (In the non-Hausdorff case, the theorems are that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is compact if and only if its topology is indiscrete and that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is locally compact if and only if it is a finitary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            of indiscrete spaces.) On the other hand, a nice property of even infinite-dimensional TVSes is that they are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path-connected+space"} path-connected
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
          %p
            More classical material should be added, particularly on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+convex+spaces"} locally convex spaces
          %h3#tvses_from_a_hilbert_space_viewpoint TVSes from a Hilbert space viewpoint
          %p
            The theory of TVS can be understood as the quest to find the essence of many fundamental theorems of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "s)," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hahn%E2%80%93Banach_theorem"} Hahn–Banach Theorem
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_mapping_theorem_%28functional_analysis%29"} Open Mapping
                and
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+graph+theorem"} closed graph theorem
          %p
            A central rôle in the whole theory plays
            = succeed "," do
              %strong duality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+convex+spaces"} locally convex spaces
            and their duals. A prominent example is the definition of certain concepts by duality in the theory of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwartz+distribution"} Schwartz distribution
